Author: Hongjie Jiang [aut], Anchi Che [aut], Yanxiao Han [aut] (co-corresponding author), Yanshuo Han [aut, cre] (co-corresponding author)
Maintainer: Yanshuo Han <yanshuohan@dlut.edu.cn>
Description: Two-group analysis of unconditional arithmetic means and
    zero-aware relative variability. Mean inference uses finite-first-moment
    generalized-gamma states, with an approximation
    matching four cumulants of the pure generalized-gamma log-location law.
    Conditional first two moments represent nonzero-frequency uncertainty.
    Empirical-Bayes posterior summaries condition
    on mixing distributions estimated by marginal maximum likelihood.
    A point null and Gaussian effect law act on the mean-contrast likelihood.
    Exact generalized-gamma likelihood values are reused between endpoint fits.
    The relative-variability model jointly learns finite-second-moment
    generalized-gamma/shifted-mixture and nonzero-frequency states.
    Sample-cluster influences
    propagate parameter covariance into Studentized log-CV-squared reference
    tails and intervals. Posterior null-mass and Studentized reference bounds
    supply evaluated screening scores. Count inputs use TMM normalization; prepared
    abundances can bypass it. Gene-wise generalized-gamma fits and shape
    tests remain descriptive diagnostics. C++17/OpenMP accelerates inference.
